A||||4||ka4nkf||E-brake (need some advice)||||1||
Z||000000||ka4nkf||04-15-2001||09:44 PM||||My E-brake does not work, so I removed the e-brake spring and cable. I can pull the e-brake lever toward the rear of the car and the e-brake works. I Decided the e-brake lever was going to far forward, so I removed it and turned the cam until I had parking brakes. then when I try to replace the lever it will not go on against the stop where it should. If I turn it to where it goes then it is about a half inch from the stop and I am right back where I started. Is there a trick that I don't know or could there be something wrong with my adjuster in the caliper. BTW both rear wheels do the same. I hate to grind the lever so it will go on against the stop, but I don't know any other way.||||reg||1
Z||000001||Coop88||04-16-2001||12:26 AM||||You're talking about the lever on the caliper itself, right? Sounds like my 88. You tighten the adjuster under the car until the e-brake will engage when you pull the handle. But when you release it the lever(s) on the calipers won't go all the way back against the stop. Then when you loosen the adjuster, the levers on the calipers will go back to their stops, but the e-brake won't hold the car in place and you have to fiddle with the handle to get the brake light to go out. Does this sound familiar? I've replaced both rear calipers, springs, the adjuster, and all 3 cables. I don't know what the secret is either. I really hope someone does.||||reg||5
Z||000002||ka4nkf||04-16-2001||10:19 AM||||Coop88<BR>Sounds like you have about the same problem. What got me curious, was that I was checking my brake pedal travel and I noticed that when I pulled the e-brake lever that my pedal travel reduced about a inch. So that is why I was trying to get the E-brake to work so it would pull the piston in and reduce my pedal travel. GM had a brake recall for this problem but it was for manual transmission's only. so what are us automatic guys supposed to do? How does the automatic's pass the safety test? The brakes are the same on auto and manual. I think we need to write the deptartment of safety. ||||reg||1
Z||000003||84Bill||04-16-2001||02:28 PM||||On my 88 the left rear ebrake got hung up and would not release properly so I shot some wd40 down the cables and that seemed to help. I noticed smoke coiling off the brakes one day and thats when I discovered it. I think I may have warped the disk cuz now it has a jearky motion when I roll to a slow stop. <BR>My solution to all this was to buy an 84 SE and part (front frame damage) the 88 out  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">||||reg||1
Z||000004||rogergarrison||04-16-2001||02:31 PM||||setting the emergency brake is supposed to adjust the driving brakes, like self adjusters. I couldnt really tell you how, Ive never had a brake problem yet.||||reg||1
